# GitHub Projects

This project's project-management tool. Owns work items (GitHub Issues), milestones, and project-board state at **{{ACCOUNT}}/{{REPOSITORY}}**, board **{{PROJECT_ID}}**.

## Owned operations

- **create work item** — open a GitHub Issue with title, body, and type.
- **transition work item** — change the issue's status on the project board.
- **comment on work item** — post a threaded comment on the issue.
- **set work item field** — Priority, Size, Type, Status, or any other project field.
- **link work item to milestone** — associate the issue with a GitHub Milestone.
- **place work item on board** — add the issue to project board {{PROJECT_ID}}.
- **create milestone** — open a GitHub Milestone (used for epics).

Any practice skill that mentions one of these operations routes through this skill.

## Access

All operations go through the **`gh` CLI**, run as `.agents/scripts/gh/gh.sh`. Never run a bare `gh`. The wrapper runs `gh` as the GitHub account that this workspace names, and a bare `gh` runs as whichever account is active on the machine. Do not call the GitHub REST API with `curl`. Use `gh api` through the wrapper when no `gh` command does the job.

| Operation | Command |
|---|---|
| create work item | `.agents/scripts/gh/gh.sh issue create --repo {{ACCOUNT}}/{{REPOSITORY}} --title "…" --body-file <file> --type Feature` |
| comment on work item | `.agents/scripts/gh/gh.sh issue comment <number> --repo {{ACCOUNT}}/{{REPOSITORY}} --body-file <file>` |
| link work item to milestone | `.agents/scripts/gh/gh.sh issue edit <number> --repo {{ACCOUNT}}/{{REPOSITORY}} --milestone "<title>"` |
| create milestone | `.agents/scripts/gh/gh.sh api repos/{{ACCOUNT}}/{{REPOSITORY}}/milestones -f title="<title>" -f description="…"` |
| place work item on board, set work item field, transition work item | the `gh project` sequence below |

### Preflight (first operation of the session)

1. **Login.** Run `.agents/scripts/gh/gh.sh auth status`. If it fails, halt and report.
2. **Repository.** Verify `{{ACCOUNT}}/{{REPOSITORY}}` is accessible with `.agents/scripts/gh/gh.sh repo view {{ACCOUNT}}/{{REPOSITORY}}`. Halt if not.
3. **Board.** Verify project board `{{PROJECT_ID}}` is accessible under `{{ACCOUNT}}` with `.agents/scripts/gh/gh.sh project view {{PROJECT_ID}} --owner {{ACCOUNT}}`. Halt if not.

## Conventions

### Work item titles

Plain English descriptions of the work. **Do not** use conventional commit format (`feat(scope): ...`) — that's for commit messages and PR titles.

### Work item types

Use the native GitHub issue type field (`--type` on `gh issue create` and `gh issue edit`):

- `Feature` — new capabilities or improvements.
- `Bug` — defects and regressions.

Do not encode the type as a label.

### Work item fields

Set the project's structured fields — do not put these in the issue body:

- **Size** — `XS`, `S`, `M`, `L`, `XL` via the project's Size field.
- **Priority** — `P0`, `P1`, `P2`, `P3` via the project's Priority field.

### Project board

Every work item must be on board `{{PROJECT_ID}}` with Size and Priority set. The complete creation sequence:

#### 1. Add the issue to the board

```bash
.agents/scripts/gh/gh.sh project item-add {{PROJECT_ID}} --owner {{ACCOUNT}} --url <issue-url>
```

#### 2. Resolve the project's GraphQL node ID (once per session)

```bash
.agents/scripts/gh/gh.sh project list --owner {{ACCOUNT}} --format json
# Find the entry with "number": {{PROJECT_ID}} and copy its "id" (e.g. PVT_kwDO...).
```

#### 3. Resolve the project item ID for this issue

```bash
.agents/scripts/gh/gh.sh project item-list {{PROJECT_ID}} --owner {{ACCOUNT}} --format json
# Find the entry matching the issue and copy its "id" (e.g. PVTI_...).
```

#### 4. Resolve field and option IDs (once per session)

```bash
.agents/scripts/gh/gh.sh project field-list {{PROJECT_ID}} --owner {{ACCOUNT}} --format json
# Find the Priority field, the Size field, and the option IDs for the values you want.
```

#### 5. Set the fields

```bash
.agents/scripts/gh/gh.sh project item-edit --id <item-id> --project-id <project-node-id> \
  --field-id <priority-field-id> --single-select-option-id <option-id>

.agents/scripts/gh/gh.sh project item-edit --id <item-id> --project-id <project-node-id> \
  --field-id <size-field-id> --single-select-option-id <option-id>
```

A work item that is not on the board with Size and Priority set is considered incomplete.

### Work item lifecycle

- Move to **In Progress** when active implementation starts.
- Move to the post-PR status (often **In review** or **Ready to Test** — resolve the actual status set with `.agents/scripts/gh/gh.sh project field-list {{PROJECT_ID}} --owner {{ACCOUNT}} --format json` once per session) when implementation is complete and a PR is open. The same status survives QA approval.
- **Do not manually move to Done.** PR-merge automation moves the card to Done when the PR is merged. Manual transitions before merge bypass that gate.

### Milestones

Epics are represented as GitHub Milestones. All subordinate feature and bug work items are associated with their parent milestone.

## Composition

When a source-control tool is installed (e.g., `github-source-control`), pull requests link back to work items by referencing the work-item number in branch names and PR bodies. This skill owns the work-item side of that link — adding the work item to the board, transitioning it, commenting on it — but does **not** own PRs.

## An empty board is a claim you have to earn

A project query that returns no items usually means you cannot see the board, not that
the board is empty. Projects are org-scoped, so an unauthorized token gets an empty
collection instead of an error. Repo issues still read fine, which makes the gap look
like data.

Never report a board as empty on one query.

1. Check the exit code. Never suppress the error stream on a query whose empty result
   you will act on.
2. Corroborate against a second org-scoped call. If `orgs/<org>/members` also returns
   nothing while collaborators exist, the token is not authorized.
3. If the counts disagree with reality, say the access is missing. Ask for SSO
   authorization on the organization.
4. Never write to a board you could not read. An add loop against a board that is
   already populated duplicates every item.

## Never conclude from a discarded result

Absence of output is not absence of data. Two habits produce a false zero:

- Suppressing an error stream, which turns a failure into a plausible empty result.
- Supplying a default for a missing key, which erases the last trace of it.

Do not combine them. Where a default is unavoidable, assert the shape you expected
before you act on it.

